      "side": 1,
      "message": "`dtlb_extra_inval` seems security related, and we cannot trust the host to set it? If the host wrongly sets it to false for those quirky devices for which it should be set to true, then device TLB flush done by pKVM may be insufficient?\n\nSo, pKVM should rather copy/reimplement the host\u0027s dev_needs_extra_dtlb_flush(), to set dtlb_extra_inval on its own?",
      "revId": "1fb3b1293cf2d125b01bba725c9f6b0ab2d45a1d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cf1f8659_516aba52",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 50
      },
      "lineNbr": 158,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-01T20:12:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "yeah true, I wanted to do this but could not find a good way to do this as we do not have a device layer framework in pkvm. During pkvm initialization, we could go through the PCI devices and create a snapshot of existing devices, but then we leave it vulnerable for hotplug devices. For 6.18, I was planning to have a thin device layer that caches device specific details that are security related.\n\nFor 6.12, would it make sense to do it always - this is not on a hot path(context tear down and pasid table tear down) so it should be okay I guess. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "ac0603a5_df3b78a8",
      "revId": "1fb3b1293cf2d125b01bba725c9f6b0ab2d45a1d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "09fd7ee3_2f07d3fa",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 50
      },
      "lineNbr": 158,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-01T20:36:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Reading more about QAT(Quick Assist Technology) devices which is what this quirk is targeting, looks like they are server grade devices embedded in xeon processors. Google also tells me there exists addon cards as well, but not sure if it will affect us. So for 6.12, I think both routes should be fine for 6.12 - always double flush for devices in SATC(not a huge perf hit I guess) or ignore it and trust the host?",
      "parentUuid": "cf1f8659_516aba52",
      "revId": "1fb3b1293cf2d125b01bba725c9f6b0ab2d45a1d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79a22eb3_f69678b5",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 50
      },
      "lineNbr": 158,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-01T20:49:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e but then we leave it vulnerable for hotplug devices\n\nLuckily in this case it seems we don\u0027t need to worry about hotplug devices. Device TLB is only allowed for SATC devices anyway, and S in SATC stands for \"SoC-integrated\".\n\n\u003e For 6.12, would it make sense to do it always - this is not on a hot path(context tear down and pasid table tear down) so it should be okay I guess.\n\nContext/pasid teardown is not the only case when dtlb_extra_inval is used. It is also used in pkvm_cache_tag_flush_range() -\u003e cache_tag_flush_devtlb_psi() and that is a hot path, right?\n\nAnyway, for 6.12, I guess at least it simply doesn\u0027t make sense to pass this parameter in the host\u0027s hypercalls (since that is not trusted anyway)? we may just never do this extra validation?\n\nFor 6.18, we _might_ consider checking dev_needs_extra_dtlb_flush() for SATC devices during pKVM initialization (and, for example, just failing pKVM initialization if it returns true for any of them, since we are unlikely to actually care about such devices)...",
      "parentUuid": "cf1f8659_516aba52",
      "revId": "1fb3b1293cf2d125b01bba725c9f6b0ab2d45a1d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
